  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Beyond pigment, cortisol, and appetite, the melanocortin system has a fourth job: dialling down inflammation. α-MSH is one of the body’s own anti-inflammatory signals.',
      'The effect runs mainly through MC1R and MC3R on immune cells such as macrophages and neutrophils — not the MC2R cortisol route — so it can calm inflammation without acting as a steroid.',
      'Melanocortins don’t only switch inflammation off; MC3R signalling actively drives its resolution, helping macrophages clear dead cells and return tissue to baseline.',
      'This is old and new at once: ACTH-based therapy has treated inflammatory disease since the 1950s, partly through steroid-independent melanocortin signalling.',
      'A modern pipeline targets the axis directly — oral MC1R agonists for ulcerative colitis (PL-8177), a melanocortin eye drop for dry eye (PL-9643), biased MC1R/MC3R agonists for arthritis (resomelagon), and MC1R agonism for the fibrosis of systemic sclerosis (dersimelagon).',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The melanocortin system is best known for the three jobs it advertises on the surface:
    it sets <a href="/receptors/mc1r">pigment</a>, drives the <a href="/acth">cortisol stress
    axis</a>, and tunes <a href="/receptors/mc4r">appetite</a>. But it has a quieter fourth
    career that the newest drugs are built on — it is one of the body’s own brakes on
    <strong>inflammation</strong>.<Cite n={1} /> The same peptide that darkens skin and
    signals fullness, <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>, is also a potent anti-inflammatory
    hormone, and it works through both glucocorticoid-dependent and glucocorticoid-independent
    routes.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>A signal that calms</h2>
  <p>
    Inflammation is the immune system’s fire alarm — useful in a crisis, damaging when it
    won’t switch off. α-MSH sits on the "off" side of that balance. It suppresses the master
    inflammatory switch <strong>NF-κB</strong>, dampening the production of the cytokines,
    nitric oxide, and adhesion molecules that recruit and activate immune cells.<Cite n={1} />
    The effect is broad: across models of gut, skin, joint, brain, and eye inflammation,
    melanocortin agonists reduce the inflammatory load — which is exactly why a "pigment and
    appetite" system keeps turning up in immunology.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>Which receptors do the work</h2>
  <p>
    Crucially, this is a <em>different</em> arm of the system from the one that makes cortisol.
    The stress axis runs through MC2R on the adrenal gland; the anti-inflammatory arm runs
    mainly through <strong>MC1R</strong> and <strong>MC3R</strong> expressed directly on immune
    cells — macrophages, monocytes, and neutrophils.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={5} /> MC1R broadly
    lowers inflammatory signalling, while MC3R was singled out early as a distinct
    anti-inflammatory receptor: synthetic MC3R agonists blunt inflammation in a way that a
    selective MC4R blocker doesn’t touch.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

