  <h2>Not just off — toward resolution</h2>
  <p>
    The most interesting part is not that melanocortins switch inflammation <em>off</em>, but
    that they help switch it <em>over</em>. Ending an inflammatory response is an active
    process — the immune system has to clear the debris and the spent cells, a step called
    <strong>resolution</strong>. Melanocortin signalling promotes it: in one study a melanocortin
    agonist increased macrophage uptake of dying neutrophils (efferocytosis) and calmed tissue,
    and that pro-resolving effect was lost in mice without MC3R but preserved without a working
    MC1R — pinning the resolution job on MC3R specifically.<Cite n={4} /> That places the
    system inside the modern field of "resolution pharmacology," which aims not to blockade the
    immune system but to help it finish the job cleanly.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>

  <ResolutionSwitch />

  <h2>The old drug that did this all along</h2>
  <p>
    None of this is entirely new to medicine — it has just been hiding in plain sight. ACTH,
    given as <a href="/therapeutics">repository corticotropin</a>, has been used against
    inflammatory disease since the 1950s, and for most of that time the assumption was that it
    worked purely by telling the adrenal gland to make cortisol. It turns out that a meaningful
    part of its benefit is <strong>steroid-independent</strong>: ACTH is itself a melanocortin,
    and it acts directly on melanocortin receptors on immune cells to calm them.<Cite n={6} />
    The old workhorse was quietly using the fourth axis the whole time — which is precisely the
    door the newer, more selective drugs are trying to walk through.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline betting on it</h2>
  <p>
    For decades this was interesting biology with no clean drug behind it. That is changing, and
    the anti-inflammatory axis now carries much of the melanocortin pipeline:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a></strong> — Palatin’s oral MC1R agonist, engineered
      to act on the colon lining without being absorbed. Its Phase 2 in ulcerative colitis
      reported clinical remission in a third of treated patients versus none on placebo, in a
      small study.<Cite n={7} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/pl-9643">PL-9643</a></strong> — a melanocortin eye drop for dry eye
      disease, applying the same tissue-calming biology straight to the ocular surface; it met
      its co-primary <em>symptom</em> endpoint in a Phase 3 trial.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resomelagon (AP1189)</strong> — an oral <a href="/effects">biased</a> MC1R/MC3R
      agonist designed to trigger anti-inflammatory signalling while sparing the pigment
      pathway. It is worth being honest here: its Phase 2b in rheumatoid arthritis
      <em>missed</em> its primary endpoint, with activity seen only in a subgroup with active
      systemic inflammation — a reminder that the frontier is real but unforgiving.<Cite n={9} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/dersimelagon">Dersimelagon</a></strong> — better known as an oral MC1R
      agonist for a pigment disorder, but also studied for the inflammation, vascular damage,
      and fibrosis of systemic sclerosis, where preclinical work showed it hitting all three at
      once.<Cite n={8} />
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Track all of them, dated and sourced, on the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    The melanocortin anti-inflammatory axis is one of the best examples of what makes this
    system worth watching: a single family of receptors, reused across organs, that can quiet
    inflammation without the blunt cost of general immunosuppression — and that can, at least in
    principle, be separated from the tanning and appetite effects that come bundled with it. The
    caution is equally real: most of these programmes are early, one flagship arthritis trial has
    already missed, and "promising in a small study" is not "proven." That is the frontier, stated
    plainly. For the underlying biology, start with <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> and the
    <a href="/system">system overview</a>.
  </p>

